The CTW Social platform constitutes a novel System and Method for Multi-Layer Input Provenance Enforcement in a Web Application Architecture, covering six distinct patentable subsystems that work together as a unified provenance enforcement stack:
Subsystem 1 — Client-Side Length Provenance Guard (OOB Guard JS)
The browser-resident OOB module implementing the same INTERNAL/VALIDATED/CLAMPED/REJECTED taxonomy as the kernel patent (19/466,387) but enforced at the JavaScript execution boundary before any string reaches the DOM, WebSocket parser, or cryptographic function. Named bound table OOB.B as single source of truth. Self-check fires at parse time. Covers claims distinct from the kernel patent — this operates in userspace at the browser trust boundary.
Subsystem 2 — Zero-Knowledge Encrypted Messaging with Steganographic Key Transport (CTW-BSC V04)
The browser-side implementation of a four-layer cipher: AES-GCM-256 + structure-preserving shuffle + UART misframe padding + zero-width Unicode Morse steganographic key transport. The server never receives the passphrase. The ciphertext blob carries its own key recovery material encoded in invisible Unicode characters invisible to any inspection layer. Novel over all prior art.
Subsystem 3 — PSN Unicode Provenance Sanitizer
The SANITIZE module implementing Pacified String Notation — a forensic record format (PSN1:TYPE:HEX:DESC:RISK) emitted at detection time for RTLO, BiDi, zero-width, and homoglyph characters. Developed in response to an observed live RTLO exploit firing inside a rendered response during a security session. Novel: the forensic record format is generated client-side before any string reaches rendering, storage, or logging, with provenance classification mirroring the OOB Guard taxonomy.
Subsystem 4 — HID Input Provenance Enforcement
The hid_provenance.py module implementing 1:1 physical-path-to-slot binding for HID devices, pre-registered virtual keyboard slots that cannot be claimed by real USB devices, BUS_VIRTUAL rejection, composite interface anomaly detection (the 258a:1006 four-interface signature), and timing-based AMT KVM injection detection. Novel: no deployed system classifies HID input by physical bus path provenance before forwarding events to applications.
Subsystem 5 — Filename Provenance Guard
The filename_provenance.py + SANITIZE.checkFilename() client/server pair implementing RTLO reversal simulation, case collision detection across filesystem boundaries, homoglyph skeleton normalization (Unicode UTS #39), zero-width char detection, and inotify-based real-time filesystem quarantine. Novel: the visual/actual filename divergence display in PSN records (what the user sees vs what the byte sequence is) combined with automatic quarantine is not present in any deployed security tool.
Subsystem 6 — System-Wide Unicode Input Provenance Layer
The unicode_guard.py FastAPI middleware implementing four-state disposition (PASS/REPLACE/STRIP/BLOCK) mirroring the OOB Guard provenance taxonomy across all HTTP, WebSocket, database, subprocess, environment variable, and log message input channels. Self-check fires at construction. Novel: the consistent four-state taxonomy applied uniformly across every ingress channel in a web application stack, with PSN-format violation records linking to the same forensic log as the kernel-level OOB Guard.
